# Simplify before splitting

A long file is not automatically a split. First ask whether the code
can be **reduced** without changing behavior or violating other rules:
duplication, unused paths, extra curry/wrappers, work the caller already
does.

Then give each remaining job a **name** at module scope. Do not leave
50–100 line closures nested inside the entry function (`renderReviewColumns`)
just to close over locals — pass a ctx object. Mutual recursion is fine
(`renderReviewItem` ↔ `renderReviewAppendix`).

Split into another file **only** when a responsibility has clear bounds
and a reason to change on its own. Splitting to “organize” or to hide
typing noise is the same smell as a zero-responsibility function.

**Do not fuse independent jobs** into one helper because they run in
sequence at one call site. Membership (“is this row in the review?”) and
identity (“panel-instance id suffix”) are two reasons to change — name
each and leave the sequence at the caller.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — extract a file that only re-exports the same walk
// ❌ BAD — keep a fallback UI the host never uses “just in case”
// ❌ BAD — 80-line `const renderItem = () => { … }` buried in the entry
// ❌ BAD — one fn owns “which rows” AND “instance ids”
visibleReviewItems(items, idSuffix, isAnswered)

// ✅ GOOD — named module-scope jobs; keep the recursive walk together
const renderReviewItem = (walk, item, …) => { … };
const renderReviewAppendix = (walk, originId) => { … };

// ✅ GOOD — two named jobs; caller composes
items
  .filter((item) => usefulForReview(item, isAnswered))
  .map((item) => renderReviewItem(walk, withIdSuffix(item, idSuffix), …))

// ✅ GOOD — a real second job (e.g. overlay submit) lives in its own file
```

Related: `no-zero-responsibility.mdc`.
